※ pl4g-test: compile=error
※ pl4g-test: expect-diag 3052 LANG_OPNAME_NOT_AN_OPERATOR

※ `←` binds a name rather than working on values, so a function that ran when a
※ name was bound would make a program's assignments into calls.  Unicode calls
※ it a symbol all the same, which is why the rule has a second half: a glyph the
※ language has given a meaning that is not an operator is not one either.

fn `←`(a: u8, b: u8) → u8:
    a

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    0u6
